<figure class="lms-figure" aria-labelledby="fig-pci">
  <figcaption id="fig-pci" class="lms-figure-caption">What touches card data, and what never should</figcaption>
  <div class="lms-fork">
    <div class="lms-fork-branch lms-fork-branch-safe">
      <p class="lms-fork-title">Built to handle card data</p>
      <ul class="lms-fork-list"><li><strong>The P18 terminal</strong>: reads and encrypts the chip.</li><li><strong>Dejavoo's cloud</strong> and <strong>Authorize.net</strong>: carry it to the processor.</li><li><strong>The processor, networks and banks.</strong></li></ul>
    </div>
    <div class="lms-fork-branch lms-fork-branch-never">
      <p class="lms-fork-title">Must never hold card data</p>
      <ul class="lms-fork-list"><li><strong>Rupert</strong>: keeps only the last four, the brand and an approval code.</li><li><strong>Our iPads, phones, email, text messages, Team chat.</strong></li><li><strong>Paper</strong>: sticky notes, order forms, the back of a receipt.</li><li><strong>People</strong>: nobody reads a card number aloud or writes one down.</li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</figure>
